body:
Many applications need to maintain compatibility with older browsers, but using native modules from workers is attractive, if only for the improved debugging experience. To support both, developers could probably use the original source wherever modules are supported, and a transpiled version otherwise.

The current spec doesn't make it very easy to check if workers support modules. In order to test for support, I think you have to create a worker and listen to its resulting message or error. That might look something like this:

```js
function supportsWorkerModule () {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const source = 'export {}; self.postMessage(); close()'
    const input = URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([source], {type: 'application/javascript'}))
    const worker = new Worker(input, {type: 'module'})
    worker.onmessage = resolve
    worker.onerror = reject
  }).then(() => true, () => false)
}

async function createWorker () {
  if (await supportsWorkerModule()) {
    return new Worker('./original.js', {type: 'module'})
  } else if (window.Worker) {
    return new Worker('./transpiled.js', {type: 'classic'})
  } else {
    throw new Error('Worker not supported')
  }
}

```

That approach doesn't seem very friendly to me. Among the issues here, I spotted the idea of adding a new class, called something like `WorkerModule`. Taking this path would make branching and selecting the correct worker type much easier:

```js
function createWorker () {
  if (window.WorkerModule) {
    return new WorkerModule('./original.js')
  } else if (window.Worker) {
    return new Worker('./transpiled.js')
  } else {
    throw new Error('Worker not supported')
  }
}
```

Workers are already a bit tedious to use. I'm worried people will be put-off further by having to jump through any extra hoops to make things work for everyday users.
